that's not part of --

00:06:24.667 --> 00:06:26.000
You do those little bits.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:27.834
But who came up with
the idea for this?

00:06:27.834 --> 00:06:29.634
-It was Alan Yang
and Matt Hubbard,

00:06:29.634 --> 00:06:32.501
who I worked with
on a show called "Forever"

00:06:32.501 --> 00:06:33.801
with our buddy Fred Armisen.

00:06:33.801 --> 00:06:36.767
And they did "30 Rock"
and "Parks and Rec"

00:06:36.767 --> 00:06:38.567
and they're great, great, great
writers.

00:06:38.567 --> 00:06:42.167
And they just said to me like,
"How would you like to play

00:06:42.167 --> 00:06:44.567
this woman
who is married to, like,

00:06:44.567 --> 00:06:46.667
the most powerful man in the
world, and they get divorced.

00:06:46.667 --> 00:06:49.167
They don't have a prenup,
so she's left with $87 billion."

00:06:49.167 --> 00:06:50.200
-Billion dollars.
-It's like, "Great."

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:53.400
Yeah, billion with a "B."

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:55.167
-So, $87 billion.

00:06:55.167 --> 00:06:58.334
And then she has to decide like,
"Hey, is this my life?

00:06:58.334 --> 00:07:00.400
Like, do I like
this material life,

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:01.734
all these material things?"

00:07:01.734 --> 00:07:03.400
-Yeah, or just, like --
I've been thinking about it

00:07:03.400 --> 00:07:04.501
a lot because of the show.

00:07:04.501 --> 00:07:08.534
Like, I think
we would all not be sad

00:07:08.534 --> 00:07:11.100
to wake up with $87 billion.

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:12.200
But then, at the same time,

00:07:12.200 --> 00:07:13.968
what enters my mind is like,

00:07:13.968 --> 00:07:15.501
"And what are you
going to do with it?"

00:07:15.501 --> 00:07:17.834
Like, a social responsibility

00:07:17.834 --> 00:07:20.234
after you buy
like a couple islands.

00:07:20.234 --> 00:07:22.801
-Yeah. Your character
is very conflicted

00:07:22.801 --> 00:07:25.100
because she is
used to living that life

00:07:25.100 --> 00:07:27.868
